      "theme": "Industrial automation, division of labour, and hygiene in modern consumer packaging.",
      "moral": "Standardized assembly lines dramatically increase efficiency and accessibility of daily necessities.",
      "intro": "Unit 14C illustrates the technological principles of the assembly line and mechanical mass production inside a British industrial biscuit bakery.",
      "fullText": "Before the invention of modern machinery, almost every article required by human beings was fashioned by hand by a skilled craftsman. This method was slow, laborious, and expensive. Only the wealthy could afford superior goods. Today, mass production has transformed human society.\n\nConsider the manufacture of biscuits in a modern factory. A traveling steel conveyor band passes through a massive tunnel oven sixty meters long. Liquid dough is mixed in vast stainless-steel vats and shaped by mechanical cutters before being deposited onto the slowly moving band. As it travels through the heated tunnel, the dough is baked to perfection without human touch.\n\nUpon emerging from the cooling section, the biscuits are mechanically packed into airtight tins or moisture-proof packets. Not a single human finger touches the biscuit from raw flour to finished container. This guarantees absolute cleanliness and reduces costs, placing nutritious food within reach of millions of consumers.",
