      "fullText": "The catastrophic devastation of the Second World War convinced the civilized world that international conflict had to be outlawed. The earlier League of Nations, established after the 1914–1918 conflict, had failed because it lacked the executive authority and military mechanism to enforce its resolutions against aggressive totalitarian states.\n\nIn the spring of 1945, delegates from fifty sovereign nations assembled in San Francisco to draft the Charter of the United Nations. The preamble of this historic document declared its central purpose: \"to save succeeding generations from the scourge of war, which twice in our lifetime has brought untold sorrow to mankind.\"\n\nThe Charter established the Security Council, charged with maintaining international peace and security, and the General Assembly, where every member nation enjoys equal voice. In addition, specialized agencies such as UNESCO, WHO, and UNICEF were created to battle poverty, disease, and illiteracy, which are the root causes of international strife.",
